From c48c1506b68924ab185fea12030d0d337956281c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 08:26:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] parse, proc macros: keep a string literal's suffix and pass
 it to proc macros

termimad 0.34.1 (rhit 2.0.4) src/parse/parse_align.rs:24:

    if regex_is_match!("left"i, s) {

    error:0: compile_error! "expected `,`"

Any identifier may follow a literal in a token stream; rustc rejects it
only where the literal is parsed as an expression. lazy_regex's proc
macros read it (syn `LitStr::suffix()`) as the regex flags, `i` =
case-insensitive. mrustc's lexer ended the string at the quote, so `i`
became a separate ident, and syn, which re-lexes `Literal::to_string()`,
saw `"left" i`.

- Lexer: an identifier directly after a string, byte string or C string
  literal read from the input becomes the token's suffix
  (`Token::m_suffix`). The string of a doc comment, which comes from the
  token queue, never takes one. The parser ignores the suffix.
- Proc macros: a new token class 12 (Suffix) follows a suffixed string or
  byte string literal, in both directions. libproc_macro keeps it as
  `LiteralValue::Suffixed`, prints it after the literal, and sends it
  a back. `TokenStream::from_str` lexes it after a string. mrustc puts
  returned suffix back on its literal (one byte of lookahead), so it
  survives a proc macro that echoes its input, e.g. an attribute macro
  around a body that calls `regex_is_match!`.
- `Token::to_str` writes the suffix.

An older libproc_macro fails only on a suffixed literal, which failed
before anyway. The rlib metadata format is unchanged (a suffix inside
an exported macro_rules body is still dropped). Numeric literals keep the
old behaviour, where an unknown suffix becomes a following ident.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
